ot switched, meaning the recommendation does not fully achieve the stated consolidation objective—it reduces from 5 to 2 plans (new Aviva + retained Zurich)."],"comparisonPerformed":true,"missingInputs":[],"analysis":"The suitability report asserts three client objectives with explicit priority ranking. Objective 1 (maintain lifestyle through lifetime) is supported by the planning projections, which show cash flow remaining in surplus throughout the 60-year horizon with no required drawdowns exceeding available assets (P5, P9). Objective 2 (retire in 2 years with £1,700 net per month household income) is stated but not quantitatively validated in the evidence. The planning baseline shows total expenditure of £24,001 in the first planning year (2025, before either retirement), which does not align to a discrete £1,700/month net target. The cashflow model does not isolate the net household income available after retirement; instead, it models total money in and total money out, showing overall surplus. The stated target of '£1,700 net per month' (£20,400 per annum) is lower than the £24,001 annual expenditure modelled, but no document confirms the target has been stress-tested or validated against the proposed plan. Objective 3 (consolidation and streamlining of five separate plans) is partially addressed: the recommendation switches four smaller arrangements into a single Aviva platform but retains Zurich (£103,439, 88.5% of total pension value). The stated preference for 'streamlined' management is thus not fully met by the recommendation, which still leaves two separate providers. The report does link recommendations to objectives (P3, P4, P6: funds chosen for 'retirement objectives', consolidation into Aviva platform), but the linkage to objective 3 is incomplete because the largest holding remains outside the recommended consolidation. The retirement age discrepancy (age 75 used for yield calculations vs. 100/104 in planning projections) is a methodological choic