ultaneously recommends an ongoing adviser charge of 1% to be paid via Aviva Platform, creating a material change in charging structure not explicitly explained.","The report recommends nomination of beneficiaries completion 'after the transfer process is begun' but does not specify whether this must be completed before funds are invested, before income is drawn, or at any other defined point material to suitability."],"comparisonPerformed":true,"missingInputs":[],"analysis":"The requirement tests whether recommendations clearly state what action will be taken, when it will be taken, and by whom where that is material to suitability. The report does name each action (Retain, Encash, Switch×3), assigns responsibility (Client for Retain; Adviser for others), and provides a timescale (10-15 working days). However, material gaps exist: (1) The retention of People's Pension is assigned to the client with no implementation instruction, timescale, or stated outcome—leaving it unclear whether the client must take any action or whether it is simply to be left in place. (2) The adviser is named as responsible party but not the specific adviser entity; the Aviva terms [P5] contemplate adviser designation and deduction, but the report does not confirm whether the signing adviser will execute or whether Aviva will do so on instruction. (3) The 10-15 working day timescale is presented as global to all transfers but the Aviva terms [P7] distinguish between Business Days and non-Business Days and reserve the right to delay, making the timescale aspirational rather than guaranteed. (4) The initial adviser fee is stated to be 'paid directly by the pension schemes on transfer' but no evidence shows that each of the five schemes has been instructed to make this payment or that the fee will not fall due from the client if schemes do not pay. (5) The Scottish Widows arrangement is assigned both an encashment action and an ongoing adviser charge of 1% per annum via Aviva Platform, but [P10