ocument (P5) provides only 2020-dated AUM (18 million customers, not AUM figure), and no S&P rating or 2024 AUM data appears in the evidence pack. The cited financial strength metric cannot be corroborated from the case file.","The report recommends switching FROM an existing Aviva arrangement (EA3, 'Aviva pension my future focus consolidation') TO the same provider (Aviva) but on a different platform and in different funds. Policy Research (P13) notes the existing Aviva arrangement was rejected as 'Lifestyle, expensive, no adviser servicing', yet the report does not explicitly explain why switching to Aviva Platform Choice resolves the 'expensive' and 'no adviser servicing' criticisms — the charge comparison on page 11 shows the switch costs 4.24% more INCLUDING adviser fees, undermining the cost rationale.","The report states investment pathways were considered and rejected (P2 evidence supports this narrative). However, the report does not name or quantify the alternative investment pathways from Aviva to show why the CIP was chosen over them. The rejection narrative is generic ('tailored solution', 'more accurate reflection') rather than comparative.","Provider selection is asserted as 'most suitable' but the report does not evidence a comparison with other SIPP providers beyond Aviva. Research document (P1, P9) shows 4 platforms were identified (Aviva Platform, Elevate/Aberdeen, Aegon ARC, Wealthtime Platform) with filters applied showing 'No filters were applied' and 'No DNA applied'. The research outcome is that only Aviva Platform matched; however, the report does not explain what criteria or objectives drove the filter design or why the other three were unsuitable — the research shows shortlist of 1 from 4 but provides no comparative justification."],"comparisonPerformed":true,"missingInputs":[],"analysis":"The report provides multiple rationales linking the recommendation to client objectives (OBJ1, OBJ2, OBJ3) and states that suitability has been assessed against circumstances and retirement objectives. The Aviva Platform provider choice is supported by documented research (SIPP research report in P1, P5, P9) that identified Aviva as the recommended platform from a panel of 4 SIPP solutions. The fund selection (HSBC and CT multi-asset funds) is supported by performance data (P10, P12) and risk-profiling (Risk Rating 5 matching the client's agreed attitude to risk). The investment strategy rationale is detailed, client-specific, and linked to objectives. \n\nHowever, material gaps exist in the consistency and completeness of the justification: \n\n(1) Financial strength claim: The S&P rating 'A (Stable)' and '£407bn AUM (31 Dec 2024)' cited on page 10 are not corroborated by any document in the evidence pack. The research data (P5) is 5 years old (31 Dec 2020). This is an unsupported assertion on a material claim used to justify provider suitability.\n\n(2) Provider selection rationale incomplete: The report states Aviva is 'most suitable' but does not explain why the other three SIPP platforms identified in the research (Elevate, Aegon ARC, Wealthtime) were rejected. The research shortlist shows 1 from 4 matched; the report does not connect this to client objectives or circumstances. A whole-of-market search was performed (P5 'Whole of Market has been used'), but the filter design and outcome are not explained in the suitability narrative. This leaves provider selection appearing arbitrary rather than justified against alternatives.\n\n(3) Internal switching cost inconsistency: The existing Aviva arrangement (EA3) was lifestyle-oriented and rejected as 'expensive' and lacking adviser servicing (P13). The report recommends switching to Aviva Platform Choice on the same provider. The charge comparison (page 11, referenced in instructions) shows the recommendation is 4.24% more expensive INCLUDING adviser fees than the current combined plans. The report does not reconcile how moving to Aviva resolves the 'expensive' criticism when the numerical comparison contradicts it. This is a material contradiction between the justification (tax efficiency, competitive charges, value) and the stated charge impact.\n\n(4) Investment pathway rejection rationale generic: The report rejected investment pathways from Aviva in favour of the CIP. The evidence (P2) supports that pathways were considered and the CIP was selected for reasons of tailoring and alignment. However, the report does not quantify or compare the pathways' cost, features, or performance against the recommended CIP strategy, making the rejection narrative explanatory rather than comparative.\n\n(5) Fund selection and existing arrangement relationship: The report does not explicitly state that switching from the existing Aviva 'my future focus consolidation' (lifestyle) fund to the recommended HSBC/CT multi-asset funds is a material change in strategy. This transition is documented in the policy research (P3, P13) but is not addressed in the suitability report narrative, leaving it unclear whether the change reflects objectives or is incidental to the consolidation.\n\nThe guard on provider marketing claims applies: the report cites Aviva's strengths (financial rating, choice, expertise, charges) as absolute claims rather than comparative ones. These are asserted without evidence they are superior to competitors (Elevate, Aegon, Wealthtime). The guard on panel membership applies: the report appears to rely on documented panel/research processes (P1, P5, P9) which is legitimate, but the report itself does not state it is relying on a documented panel process — the research exists but the suitability report does not cite or explain reliance on it.\n\nThe comparison WAS performed (research documents exist, provider and fund data are present, objectives are stated), so comparisonPerformed is TRUE. The issues are gaps in explanation and evidence, not absence of comparison.","citations":[{"passageId":"P1","source":"SIPP research report.md","category":"G","quote":null,"cells":["Aviva Platform","Elevate","Aegon Retirement Choices (ARC)","Wealthtime Platform","4 products from a total of 4 matched the filters applied","No filters were applied"]},{"passageId":"P5","source":"SIPP research report.md","category":"G","quote":"The Aviva brand name was launched in July 2002. The group was formed by the merger of CGU and Norwich Union in 2000. CGU came from the merger of Commercial Union and General Accident in 1998. Aviva provide savings, retirement and insurance to 18 million (as at 31st December 2020) customers in their core markets (UK & Ireland Life, General Insurance: UK & Ireland, General Insurance: Canada).","cells":null},{"passageId":"P9","source":"SIPP research report.md","category":"G","quote":"1 products from a total of 118 matched the filters applied. Pension Portfolio - Choice | Aviva Life & Pensions UK Limited","cells":null},{"passageId":"P10","source":"SIPP research report.md","category":"G","quote":null,"cells":["HSBC Global Strategy Balanced Portfolio Accumulation C","0.18%","£99.17","0.01%","£101.92","0.19%"]},{"passageId":"P12","source":"SIPP research report.md","category":"G","quote":null,"cells":["CT Universal MAP Balanced Fund C Acc (GBP)","1YR","11.17","3YR","36.66","5YR","44.73"]},{"passageId":"P13","source":"Test Case 1 - Policy Research -2025.md","category":"G","quote":"Recommendation: Switch. Why/Why isnt this scheme suitable for the client? Lifestyle, expensive, no adviser servicing","cells":null},{"passageId":"P14","source":"Pension switch report - updated.md","category":"G","quote":"The recommendation is to consolidate your existing pensions into a new solution as per the details below.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P2","source":"Suitability Report Test 1.md","category":"I","quote":"You require a more tailored solution than any of the four generic solutions offered by the provider. It does not reflect your individual circumstances, withdrawal requirements and risk profile as accurately as the recommended investment strategy.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P8","source":"Key Features (1).md","category":"H","quote":"It's a self-invested personal pension (SIPP) providing a flexible, tax-efficient retirement plan for UK residents aged 18 or over. Pension Portfolio is provided through the Aviva Platform.","cells":null}],"issueCategories":["Weak justification","Missing component"],"severity":"Moderate","outcome":"PotentialConcern"}

