e' — same recommendation is simultaneously cheaper and more expensive depending on adviser fees, indicating the cost comparison is incomplete without explicit reconciliation of both layers"],"comparisonPerformed":false,"missingInputs":["Explicit charge-by-charge reconciliation showing how the 0.44% total charge (0.21% platform + 0.22% investment management) applies to the consolidated transfer value of £116,998.47 or to the individual arrangement values being consolidated","Clarification of whether the £268.94 comparison row for Standard Life should have been calculated on Standard Life's £3,002.00 value (implying 0.26% cost = £7.81 difference) or whether the row actually references Zurich","Aggregated all-in cost figure showing cumulative adviser fees (£3,305 initial + 1.00% ongoing), product charges, platform charges, and fund charges combined, with net cost-benefit analysis across the projection horizon","Documented adviser fee schedule confirming whether the 1.00% ongoing charge stated in the report matches any client agreement or terms of engagement document not provided in this pack","Detailed reconciliation of the critical yield requirement (4.8% mid scenario) back to the specific charge layers and switching costs, demonstrating how this figure was derived from the charge data"],"analysis":"The requirement is not met. Multiple material inconsistencies prevent reliable comparison of cumulative cost impact. First, the Standard Life charge contradiction: the report states 0.52% on page 5 but uses 0.18% on page 11. These cannot both represent the full recurring charge, and no explanation is given for the divergence. The extraction metadata notes this ambiguity but does not resolve it. Second, the derived figures reveal systematic misalignment between stated monetary charges and the fund values they purport to apply to. The platform and investment management charges are quoted in pounds but calculate backwards to fund values (£110k-£115k) that do not correspond to