harge percentages do not match the totals: the Aviva platform charge of £231.39 at 0.21% implies a fund value of £110,185.71, which matches neither the individual arrangement values nor the total £116,998.47. This undermines confidence in the switching-effect calculations.","P1 and P2 both state growth assumptions are 0.00%, 2.95%, and 5.88%, but P1 also references additional scenarios ('Scottish Widows only', 'Zurich only', 'Standard Life only') with different figures, whereas the report asserts a single unified comparison. The evidence contains multiple switching scenarios, but the report does not explain which existing arrangements are being compared in the headline figures."],"comparisonPerformed":true,"missingInputs":[],"analysis":"The comparison can be performed because RIY and critical yield figures from the report (P17, P16) are directly stated in the supporting Pension Switch Report (P2). However, a critical problem emerges: two different versions of the pension switch illustration exist (P1 and P2), presenting fundamentally different cost profiles for the same recommendation. P1 shows a recommended RIY of only 0.6% across all scenarios, implying minimal cost impact; P2 shows 2.1%–2.3%, implying substantial cost impact. The report's figures align exactly with P2, but P1 is equally present in the evidence pack and presents a contradictory cost picture. This is not a rounding difference or a scenario distinction — it is a material variance (0.6% vs 2.1% in the Low scenario) in the core cost metric that drives the switching recommendation. The guard states that RIY should be compared only at the same growth basis, and that critical yield figures must be at the same retirement age. Both conditions are met here: all figures are at age 75 and aligned by growth-rate scenario. However, the guard does not excuse two different supporting illustrations with conflicting results. The report cites one (implicitly, through exact figure matching with P2) but does not address the existence of P1 or explain why its lower cost figures should be disregarded. Additionally, the Aviva platform illustration (P13) states that 'All charges could reduce this pension fund from £129,000.00 to £115,000.00 — this is the same as reducing the investment growth after inflation from 2.9% to 0.8%. This is a reduction in the growth rate of 2.1%.' This 2.1% figure matches the report's Low-scenario recommended RIY (P17), not the Mid-scenario 2.2% or High-scenario 2.3%. The Mid scenario, which carries the critical yield of 4.8% and is described in the report as the primary basis for the recommendation (P16: 'Based on a mid-growth rate of 2.94%...'), does not align with any illustration's stated all-in cost reduction. The derived figures show that the Aviva platform charge of 0.21% and investment charges of 0.22% sum to 0.43%, which rounds to 0.44% (stated in P12 and consistent with the report's recommendation summary), but this total does not reconcile with the 2.2% RIY claimed for the Mid scenario in P17. A 0.44% platform-and-fund charge cannot produce a 2.2% reduction in yield unless additional costs (adviser fees, transfer costs, or opportunity costs) are embedded in the RIY figure without disclosure. The report states (P16) that the required outperformance is £4,672 in monetary terms, which the derived figures confirm is computed at 4.24% against a fund value of £110,188.68 — close to but not exactly matching any single existing arrangement. This suggests the comparison may be aggregating multiple plans, but the report does not clearly state which arrangements are being switched. The presence of two different switch-comparison illustrations (P1 and P2), the misalignment between the Mid-scenario RIY (2.2%) and the Aviva platform illustration's all-in cost reduction (2.1%), and the ambiguity around which existing arrangements form the comparison baseline all point to incomplete or inconsistent documentation. The report's own figures are internally consistent with P2 and P17, but the evidence pack contains a contradictory first version (P1) that is not reconciled or explained.","citations":[{"passageId":"P17","source":"Suitability Report Test 1.md","category":"I_RecommendationsAndAdvice","quote":"Reduction in yield [1]\n0.3%\n2.1%\n0.3%\n2.2%\n0.3%\n2.3%","cells":null},{"passageId":"P2","source":"Pension switch report - updated.md","category":"G","quote":"Reduction in yield\n0.3%\n2.1%\n0.3%\n2.2%\n0.3%\n2.3%","cells":null},{"passageId":"P16","source":"Suitability Report Test 1.md","category":"I_RecommendationsAndAdvice","quote":"Based on a mid-growth rate of 2.94%, including our advice fees, the recommended plan would need to grow by 4.8% per year up to age 75 in order to meet the same value as your current plan.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P1","source":"Pension switch report - updated.md","category":"G","quote":"Reduction in yield\n0.3%\n0.6%\n0.3%\n0.6%\n0.3%\n0.6%","cells":null},{"passageId":"P13","source":"Illustration - new.md","category":"H","quote":"All charges could reduce this pension fund from £129,000.00 to £115,000.00 — this is the same as reducing the investment growth after inflation from 2.9% to 0.8%. This is a reduction in the growth rate of 2.1%.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P12","source":"Illustration - new.md","category":"H","quote":"CT Universal MAP Balanced C Acc GB00BF99W060\n0.29%\nHSBC Global Strategy Balanced Portfolio C Acc GB00B76WP695\n0.18%\nWeighted Average charge\n0.23%","cells":null},{"passageId":"P2","source":"Pension switch report - updated.md","category":"G","quote":"Critical yield\n-\n1.9%\n-\n4.8%\n-\n7.8%","cells":null}],"issueCategories":["Data inconsistency","Weak justification"],"severity":"High","outcome":"PotentialConcern"}

