{"groupId":"CHK-008","requirement":"[G8.3] Tax consequences of the recommendation — Limb: Consistency. The suitability report must fairly, clearly, accurately and prominently explain all material tax consequences of the recommendations, and these explanations must be consistent with the supporting evidence provided.","reportSays":"The report states seven tax consequences: (1) Small pots taxation: '25% taken as tax-free cash and the remaining 75% taxed as income at your marginal tax rate' with estimated net receipt of £170 on a £200 fund (page 6). (2) Personal allowance optimisation: 'If you were to leave this pot to grow until you retire, you could potentially take the full amount within your personal allowance, meaning no tax would be due' (page 6). (3) Tax-free lump sum: 'An individual can take up to 25% of the pension fund being crystallised as a tax-free lump sum at outset (subject to the Lump Sum Allowance of £268,275)' (page 19). (4) Flexi-access drawdown taxation: 'The remainder of the fund remains invested within a tax advantaged environment...each withdrawal is taxed at their marginal rate of Income Tax' (page 19). (5) Death before age 75: 'all these options are paid free of tax' (page 9). (6) Death after age 75: 'tax is paid at the beneficiary's marginal rate of tax' (page 9). (7) IHT change from 6 April 2027: 'most unused defined contribution pensions and death benefits...will be included in your taxable estate for IHT' with exceptions listed (page 9). Current IHT liability stated as £0 (page 3).","fileSays":"The supporting evidence corroborates most tax consequences through Key Features [P2] and [P4]. [P2] states: 'You can normally withdraw up to 25% of your pension fund as a tax-free lump sum' and 'You may have to pay income tax on any income payments...the tax you pay will depend on your personal circumstances'; it also references HMRC lump sum allowance and lump sum & death benefit allowance limits, advising 'You should always consult your adviser for up-to-date information relating to your personal circumstances.' [P4] confirms: 'If you die before you're 75, we'll normally pay any benefits tax free to your nominated beneficiaries' and 'If you die after the age of 75...Any benefits will be taxed at your beneficiaries' marginal rate.' [P4] also addresses the change to IHT treatment. However, the client's marginal tax rate is not established in the evidence. The report does not disclose the client's current tax position, income tax band, or how the 75% of the Scottish Widows encashment (£158.49 gross) would be taxed at the client's specific marginal rate. The £170 net estimate appears to assume a tax rate but this is not made explicit or justified against the client's financial position.","discrepancies":["The report estimates Scottish Widows net encashment receipt of £170 from a £211.32 fund (25% tax-free = ~£53, 75% = ~£158.49). This implies a tax rate of approximately 17–20% on the 75% portion, but the report does not state the client's marginal tax rate or justify this estimate against the client's actual income position documented as £300/week net HGV driver income plus £20/week volunteer income, plus spouse income of £1,100/month net (page 5). The tax consequence is stated without the client's tax band being established.","Tax-free lump sum consequence (25%) is stated generically with reference to the Lump Sum Allowance (£268,275) but no assessment is made of whether the client's total pension funds (£116,998.47 across five arrangements) will trigger this allowance or whether any prior use of the allowance exists, making the stated consequence unverifiable for this client.","The report states IHT liability is currently £0 (page 3) but does not address the material tax consequence of the 6 April 2027 change: after that date, the remaining drawdown funds (estimated at retirement as between £97,600 and £150,000 depending on growth, per page 11) will be in the client's taxable estate unless passed to a surviving spouse, dependent, or via death-in-service. For an estate planning perspective, this is a material disadvantage or risk of the recommendation (choosing drawdown over annuity) that is disclosed in generic form but not explained for this client's circumstances."],"comparisonPerformed":true,"missingInputs":["Client's marginal income tax rate or tax band (required to verify the £170 net estimate on Scottish Widows encashment and to assess tax consequences on future drawdown withdrawals).","Confirmation of whether the client has previously used any of the Lump Sum Allowance or Lump Sum & Death Benefit Allowance.","Confirmation of the client's spouse's tax position and whether joint IHT planning or spouse exemptions are relevant to the 6 April 2027 change."],"analysis":"Comparison can be performed on most stated tax consequences. The report's core tax statements on small pots, tax-free lump sum, flexi-access drawdown taxation, and death benefit tax treatment are supported by Key Features evidence [P2] and [P4]. However, two material gaps emerge when read against the decision logic for 'No Issue' (evidence complete, consistent and supports outcome; material disadvantages, product and investment risks, tax consequences and at least one loss scenario stated in the report, consistent with the research and product literature, and presented where the client will see them). First, the client's marginal tax rate is not established anywhere in the evidence pack, yet the report uses it to estimate the net receipt from encashing Scottish Widows (£170 net on £211.32 gross, implying ~17% average tax on the 75% portion). This is an assertion without foundation: a tax consequence stated without the client's tax band established is unverifiable, not necessarily wrong (per the guard). Second, the 6 April 2027 IHT change is disclosed on page 9 in generic form, but the report does not explain that the recommended solution (flexi-access drawdown with funds remaining invested and passed on death) will bring the client's pension into their taxable estate from that date, whereas the existing annuity option or full encashment would not. This is a material tax consequence of the recommendation — a disadvantage of choosing drawdown over alternatives — that is mentioned generically but not explained for this client's circumstances or presented prominently in the recommendation section. The report does address the £268,275 lump sum allowance limit but does not assess whether the client's £116,998 across five arrangements poses any risk to this limit when consolidated and drawn, nor does it address the separate lump sum & death benefit allowance of £1,073,100. The Scottish Widows net estimate of £170 sits between the 20% and 40% tax rates; without the client's actual tax position, this cannot be verified as consistent. The report does not state explicitly whether this is net of the 20% or 40% higher rate, nor does it address the recent inflation in the client's circumstances (page 5 shows £300/week HGV income + £20/week volunteer + £1,100/month spouse income), which might affect the marginal rate calculation.","citations":[{"passageId":"P2","source":"Key Features (1).md","category":"H","quote":"You can normally withdraw up to 25% of your pension fund as a tax-free lump sum. You may have to pay income tax on any income payments and lump sum payments you take.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P2","source":"Key Features (1).md","category":"H","quote":"The tax benefits of any investment will depend on your personal circumstances and tax laws. You should always consult your adviser for up-to-date information relating to your personal circumstances.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P4","source":"Key Features (1).md","category":"H","quote":"If you die before you're 75, we'll normally pay any benefits tax free to your nominated beneficiaries. If you die after the age of 75...Any benefits will be taxed at your beneficiaries' marginal rate.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P6","source":"Suitability Report Test 1.md","category":"I","quote":"An individual can take up to 25% of the pension fund being crystallised as a tax-free lump sum at outset (subject to the Lump Sum Allowance of £268,275 for people with no form of lifetime allowance protection). The remainder of the fund remains invested within a tax advantaged environment from which the individual can take regular or ad-hoc income withdrawals to suit their needs and circumstances. However, each withdrawal is taxed at their marginal rate of Income Tax.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P10","source":"Suitability Report Test 1.md","category":"I","quote":"Under the small pot rules, a £200 pension fund withdrawal would be taxed with 25% taken as tax-free cash and the remaining 75% taxed as income at your marginal tax rate. We estimate you would receive £170 net.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P3","source":"Suitability Report Test 1.md","category":"I","quote":"Currently, your Inheritance Tax liability will be £0.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P5","source":"Suitability Report Test 1.md","category":"I","quote":"You are currently aged 69 and married to Kim, aged 65. You work full-time as an HGV driver through an agency, typically earning around £300 per week net, with additional income of £20 per week from your volunteer role with the Sea Cadets. Kim earns approximately £1,100 per month net.","cells":null}],"issueCategories":["Disclosure shortfall","Data quality"],"severity":"Moderate","outcome":"PotentialConcern"}

