s not state that the existing Zurich arrangement (EA4, current value £103,439.24) does not offer FAD at all without fund value increase to £10k minimum ([P23]), whereas the recommended Aviva platform does offer full FAD ([P23]). This is a material withdrawal feature gain, not discussed in the disadvantages section despite being listed as a disadvantage of the new plan.","The report states Standard Life charges 0.52% (page 5 arrangements table) but the Pension Switch Charges table (page 11) uses 0.18% as the current annual recurring charge. The derived figure analysis shows EA5 impact row is computed on the wrong plan (Zurich's £103,438.46 rather than Standard Life's £3,002.00). Any cost comparison resting on this row is unreliable, yet the report cites it to support switching advantages.","The report does not disclose that the existing Aviva fund is a lifestyling/consolidation fund ('Aviva pension my future focus consolidation' [P20]) which automatically reduces risk approaching retirement, whereas the recommended HSBC Global Strategy and CT Universal MAP Balanced funds are static risk rating 5 allocations. The loss of automatic de-risking is a material disadvantage affecting long-term retirement adequacy, particularly for a client now aged 55 heading to retirement at 75.","The report states the recommendation is 'Like for Like 0.24% less expensive' but 'Including Adviser Fee 4.24% more expensive' (page 11). This fundamental tension—that the switch requires 4.8% critical yield to break even once advice costs are included—is disclosed but not highlighted as a material disadvantage requiring explicit discussion of the outcome if markets underperform.","The report does not quantify the projected retirement value reduction illustrated in the Pension Switch Report comparative tables ([P2] showing £10 or 0.3-0.2% decrease in maturity value). While small in percentage terms, the report presents no loss scenario illustrating actual fund value impact in monetary terms at retirement under base-case or downside assumptions.","The Key Features [P4] warn that income drawdown depletes funds if investments do not grow sufficiently and that high income withdrawals leave less for dependants or annuity purchase. The report recommends drawdown from the Scottish Widows small pot (encashment, not drawdown) but does not discuss drawdown sustainability post-switch for the consolidated £110k+ fund, representing a material consumer harm scenario if income needs exceed sustainable withdrawal rates."],"comparisonPerformed":true,"missingInputs":[],"analysis":"The report discloses multiple disadvantages and limitations spread across its narrative and appendix sections, consistent with the guard that disadvantages may be distributed rather than collected. However, material disadvantages identified in the supporting evidence are not fairly disclosed: (1) The cost basis used to support the switching recommendation appears unreliable—the Standard Life charge comparison in the Pension Switch Report uses 0.18% recurring charge (page 11 [P2]) contradicting the 0.52% stated in the report's own arrangements table (page 5), and the derived figures show the impact row is computed on Zurich's value rather than Standard Life's, undermining the 0.24% cost saving claim. (2) The loss of automatic lifestyling from the existing Aviva consolidation fund to static risk 5 allocations is material for a 55-year-old client retiring at 75, but is not disclosed as a disadvantage of the new solution. (3) The report discloses the adviser fee cost (4.24% additional cost when included) and critical yield requirement (4.8%) but does not prominently present this as a material loss scenario or disadvantage—the recommendation requires market outperformance to justify costs, yet no downside scenario illustrates what happens if this does not occur. (4) The withdrawal flexibility disadvantage is misdirected: the report states the new Aviva plan limits flexibility, but the existing Zurich plan (the largest holding) offers no FAD unless fund value reaches £10k minimum ([P23]), whereas the new Aviva plan offers full FAD. The report thus presents as a disadvantage what may be an improvement for the primary asset. (5) The Pension Switch Report explicitly warns 'A switch between pension providers does not guarantee a greater pension fund at retirement' ([P20]), quantifying small projected losses (£10 decrease, [P2]), yet the suitability report does not restate this in a material loss scenario section. (6) Income drawdown sustainability post-switch is not addressed despite Key Features warnings ([P4]) that high withdrawals deplete funds and leave less for dependants, a material consumer harm scenario absent from the report. The tax consequence of the small pot encashment (75% at marginal rate) is stated but the estimated net receipt (£170 from £211.32 gross) assumes a tax rate not explicitly disclosed in the tax treatment section. The market timing and access delay risks are disclosed but presented generically; no product-specific discussion connects them to the specific funds recommended (HSBC Global Strategy, CT Universal MAP). The requirement to assess consistency with supporting evidence is partially met: stated disadvantages are consistent with general product literature, but material disadvantages unique to this client's switch (cost basis reliability, lifestyling loss, withdrawal feature mismatch, fee-driven critical yield) are incompletely or inaccurately disclosed.","citations":[{"passageId":"P20","source":"Suitability Report Test 1.md","category":"I","quote":"A switch between pension providers does not guarantee a greater pension fund at retirement and there is no guarantee that the new provider's funds will outperform those of your existing provider.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P2","source":"Pension switch report - updated.md","category":"G","quote":null,"cells":["Effect of switching","£10 or 0.3% decrease","High growth rate (5.88%)"]},{"passageId