ction: 'Zurich does not offer risk level 5 funds, so to achieve this profile, a blend of a risk level 7 and a risk level 2 fund has been used. As there are no multi-asset funds available within your risk range, the blend consists of the Managed AP fund (in which you are already invested) and an asset-specific fund, Interest Deposits. We would not recommend investing in single asset types, as this reduces diversification, and the blended funds have not performed as well as the options available through Aviva, albeit marginally. For these reasons, we would not recommend an internal fund switch, as your main priority is to consolidate your pensions, gain better control over them, and be well-positioned for retirement with all available drawdown options.' The pension switch report [P9], [P12], [P13], [P16], [P18], [P19] shows detailed charge and maturity value comparisons for all three switching scenarios (Aviva only, Standard Life only, Zurich only, and consolidated), with critical yield analysis and effect of switching calculations. However, no documented consideration of alternatives such as: retaining all pensions as-is without any switching; retaining some pensions and switching others on an individual basis (other than what was recommended); consolidating into a different platform or provider; or phased switching to mitigate timing risk.","discrepancies":["The report states that only two alternatives (internal fund switches for Aviva and Zurich) were considered and rejected. No evidence in the file documents consideration of the alternative to take no action at all—that is, retaining all existing arrangements unchanged. While the switch report [P9] compares switching against retention implicitly in its 'effect of switching' calculations, there is no explicit statement in the suitability report that the option of doing nothing was considered and compared.","The report does not document consideration of alternative consolidation targets. The switch report [P9] shows