io against the existing one, and these show losses of varying magnitude (£3,000–£15,000 depending on growth rate for the three-plan consolidation at [P13]), yet the suitability report does not discuss whether these detriments were outweighed by non-quantified benefits such as consolidation, control, and drawdown flexibility. The Zurich rejection states performance of the blended funds 'have not performed as well as the options available through Aviva, albeit marginally' but no comparative performance data is cited. The guard (Options assessed once for the whole case may legitimately cover several plans — check whether the rejection reasoning is plan-specific where the plans differ materially) does apply: the Aviva and Zurich rejections are materially different (lifestyling drift vs. limited fund range) and therefore plan-specific, which is appropriate. However, the absence of an explicit documented consideration of the no-action baseline, and the absence of performance substantiation for the Zurich claim, means the alternatives analysis is incomplete. The suitability report's statement of considered alternatives is partially supported but not comprehensively evidenced.","citations":[{"passageId":"P1","source":"Suitability Report Test 1.md","category":"I","quote":"I do not recommend that you consolidate into this plan as the funds do not meet your risk profile; Currently, Scottish Widows does not offer a single multi-asset fund that aligns with your risk rating of 5.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P6","source":"Suitability Report Test 1.md","category":"I","quote":"Zurich does not offer risk level 5 funds, so to achieve this profile, a blend of a risk level 7 and a risk level 2 fund has been used. As there are no multi-asset funds available within your risk range, the blend consists of the Managed AP fund (in which you are already invested) and an asset-specific fund, Interest Deposits. We would not recommend investing in single asset types, as this reduces diversificat