harge statement is ambiguous (0.52% vs 0.18%), affecting the reliability of the cost comparison that justifies switching that plan. Finally, the report's treatment of alternatives is selective: it lists four main options and rejects three with stated reasons, but for Standard Life and Scottish Widows it provides shorter narratives. Scottish Widows is to be encashed (small pots), not consolidated, yet this is not framed as an alternative rejected but as a subsidiary action. The analysis does not explore whether retaining the largest three schemes (Zurich £103,439, Aviva £3,744, Standard Life £3,002 = £110,185 total) and encashing only the small pots (People's £6,601, Scottish Widows £211 = £6,812) would meet the client's objectives at lower cost and risk. In sum: alternatives were considered, rationales were given, but the switch to a consolidated single Aviva solution produces a projected 9.4% maturity value decrease versus the best alternative (retain Zurich, switch others), requires a critical yield of 4.8% p.a. to break even, and the report's claimed cost saving (0.24%) applies only before adviser fees are removed—a scenario not reflected in the stated maturity values. The weakness is not absence of comparison but the mismatch between claimed benefit (cost saving, consolidation) and evidenced outcome (maturity decrease, high critical yield).","citations":[{"passageId":"P10","source":"Suitability Report Test 1.md","category":"I","quote":"Your main priority is to consolidate your pensions, gain better control over them, and be well-positioned for retirement with all available drawdown options.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P13","source":"Pension switch report - updated.md","category":"G","quote":null,"cells":["Existing pension","£127,000","0.3%","New solution","£115,000","2.2%","Effect of switching","£12,000 or 9.4% decrease"]},{"passageId":"P13","source":"Pension switch report - updated.md","category":"G","quote":"Critical Yield is the growth rate required from the