ease at mid-growth. Critical yield required: 3.2%. [P16] Suitability Report existing arrangements table states: Aviva (SP50163762) charges '0.50% fund and £4.50 annual admin charge'; Scottish Widows (ZU4225575) charges '0.19% fund and 0.10% platform'; Zurich (P10026-719-001/DL) charges '0.93% platform'; Standard Life (D2301334000) charges '0.52% fund charge'. Retirement options: People's Pension 'FAD, Full and partial UFPLS'; Scottish Widows 'Full and partial UFPLS'; Aviva 'FAD, Full and partial UFPLS'; Zurich 'Full and partial UFPLS'; Standard Life 'FAD'. Number of funds: People's Pension 7, Scottish Widows 29, Aviva 80+, Zurich 60+, Standard Life 300. Adviser servicing: all 'No' except Standard Life (not stated in row but implied from context as having adviser capability). [P18] Aviva Policy Info confirms plan SP50163762 invests in 'Aviva Pensions My Future Focus Consolidation (Pre-2024) S2', has 'Lifestage Investment Approach', no guarantees, no Guaranteed Minimum Pension. [P6] Suitability Report performance analysis states: Aviva existing (15.58%), Zurich existing (44.32%), Standard Life (24.56%) over 5 years as at 13/10/2025. Chart shows 'The recommended Aviva advised funds have demonstrated stronger performance compared to your existing Aviva holdings. However, they have not outperformed your current Zurich funds.' [P7] Key Features document describes FAD, UFPLS, capped drawdown, annuity options available on recommended scheme. [P12] Pension Switch Report new solution details: CT Universal MAP Balanced Fund C Acc (50%) and HSBC Global Strategy Balanced Portfolio Accumulation C (50%); initial adviser charge £3,305.00; ongoing adviser charge 1.00%; immediate death benefit shortfall £3,305.00. [P3] Switch Report shows 'Existing pensions vs. New solution' comparison at medium growth (2.95%) projects existing £127,000 vs new £125,000 (effect of switching -£2,000 or -1.6% decrease). [P19] Suitability Report cashflow projections (real terms, retirement age 75): existing £127,000 vs new solution £115,000 at mid-growth (2.95%), effect of switching -£12,000 or -9.4% decrease; critical yield 4.8%. [P9] Pension Switch Report stakeholder pension comparison shows new solution projected value £125,000 vs existing solution £127,000; new solution reduction in yield 0.6% vs existing 0.3%; critical yield 3.2%. [P17] Pension Switch Report Alternative comparisons shows Aviva plan only: existing £4,280 vs new £4,260 at mid-growth (2.94%); Zurich only: existing £127,000 vs new £125,000 at mid-growth (2.95%), effect -£2,000 or -1.6% decrease; Standard Life only: existing £3,440 vs new £3,420 at mid-growth (2.95%), effect -£20 or -0.6% decrease.","discrepancies":["Aviva charge comparison: report states '0.06% more expensive' for Aviva existing (0.50%) vs new (0.44%), but 0.50% - 0.44% = 0.06% difference, which is arithmetically consistent; however, the report also assesses this as 'Better' for the new solution, which is correct directionally.","Zurich charge comparison: report asserts existing 0.93% vs recommended 0.44%, but the pension switch report [P3] shows Zurich only comparison with existing 0.29% reduction in yield (not 0.93%) at one point, creating internal inconsistency across documents.","Standard Life charges: extraction states 0.52% fund charge on page 5 of suitability report; however, the charge arithmetic analysis (derived figures) notes on page 11 (Pension Switch Charges table) the 'Current Annual Recurring Charge' appears as 0.18% with impact -0.26%, implying a confusion between gross fund charge and net recurring charge after credits.","Performance comparison: report states Zurich existing cumulative return 44.32% over 5 years; however, on page 6 of suitability report it later shows Zurich Managed Portfolio performance as 46.14% (not 44.32%), creating a date/source ambiguity.","Risk level for Aviva existing: report states 'Risk level 2; lifestyling funds' but extraction metadata notes this fund has 'Lifestage Investment Approach' which is auto-switching, not a fixed Risk level 2; the risk level drifts over time as client approaches retirement, so stating it as 'Risk level 2' is incomplete.","Retirement options for Zurich existing: report comparison states 'Full and partial UFPLS only' (no FAD), but [P1] policy research table lists Zurich as having 'Full and partial UFPLS' with FAD marked as '1' (yes), creating contradiction.","Fund range for Standard Life existing: report states 300 funds available on page 5 suitability report table [P16], but this is not reflected in the comparison dimension array, which omits Standard Life fund-range comparison despite that being a key 'Better' dimension for other arrangements.","Adviser charging capacity: report states existing arrangements do not allow adviser charging (listed as 'Does not allow adviser management' for Aviva and Zurich), but [P1] and [P16] show Standard Life as having adviser charging capability ('Yes' / 'Can adviser charging be applied: Yes'), and the recommendation notes ongoing adviser charge of 1.00%, implying the new solution does facilitate this; however, Standard Life existing was also supposed to already support this per the evidence.","Retirement options for Standard Life existing vs new: report comparison shows existing 'FAD' vs new 'Flexi Access Drawdown with enhanced flexibility', but both FAD and Flexi Access Drawdown (FAD) are the same product; the distinction drawn is unclear and potentially spurious.","Like-for-Like vs Including Adviser Fee contradiction: report page 11 states the new plan is '0.24% less expensive' without adviser fees but '4.24% more expensive' with adviser fees, meaning the switch is simultaneously cheaper and more expensive depending on fee inclusion; the report does not clearly reconcile why the recommendation stands given the advised cost is materially higher.","Projected maturity value discrepancy: suitability report [P19] at mid-growth shows existing £127,000 vs new solution £115,000 (effect -£12,000 or -9.4% decrease in real terms); however, pension switch report [P3] shows existing £127,000 vs new solution £125,000 (effect -£2,000 or -1.6% decrease). These are two different comparisons of the same scenario and cannot both be correct; the suitability report uses real-terms projections while the switch report uses nominal, but this critical difference is not flagged or explained to the client.","Critical yield inconsistency: suitability report states critical yield 4.8% per annum (page 11 Appendix); switch report shows critical yield 3.2% for the same arrangement at mid-growth; these are materially different and not reconciled.","Fund names for new solution: report lists recommended funds as 'HSBC Global Strategy Balanced Portfolio' and 'CT Universal MAP Balanced', but the switch report [P12] names them 'HSBC Global Strategy Balanced Portfolio Accumulation C (GBP)' and 'CT Universal MAP Balanced Fund C Acc (GBP)'; while these refer to the same funds, the omission of the share class detail in the comparison is incomplete.","Scottish Widows treatment: report recommendation encashes Scottish Widows at small pots (£211.32 gross → £170 net), but no comparison is provided between retaining this arrangement and encashing it; it is treated as 'Retain and Encash' per [P1] rather than switched, yet the charge/benefit analysis does not address whether encashment is justified or whether retaining in a drawdown vehicle would be better.","Performance claim vs actual outperformance: report states [P6] 'The recommended Aviva advised funds have demonstrated stronger performance compared to your existing Aviva holdings. However, they have not outperformed your current Zurich funds.' But the comparison dimensions do not include a 'Performance' row showing Zurich existing vs new solution performance, only stating that recommended funds align with risk tolerance 'with reduced volatility'; the recommendation relies on a 'comparable performance with reduced volatility' narrative without quantifying whether this trades off favorably against the 44.32% Zurich return."],"comparisonPerformed":true,"missingInputs":[],"analysis":"The requirement is to assess whether a recommendation to replace five existing pension arrangements into one new Aviva Platform solution is consistent with and supported by evidence. A comparison has been performed across multiple dimensions. However, several material inconsistencies and gaps emerge. First, the critical yield required for the new solution to match the existing arrangements is stated inconsistently: the suitability report asserts 4.8% per annum, while the pension switch report supporting the same recommendation shows 3.2% for a materially identical scenario. This is not a guard-explainable difference; both documents purport to analyze the same consolidation, and the lower critical yield in the switch report materially understates the investment growth required to justify the switch. Second, the projected maturity values diverge substantially between the two analyses: the suitability report (using real-terms projections, retirement age 75, mid-growth 2.95%) shows existing £127,000 vs new £115,000 (effect -£12,000 or -9.4% decrease), while the pension switch report (same mid-growth, same term) shows existing £127,000 vs new £125,000 (effect -£2,000 or -1.6% decrease). The suitability report's figure implies the client is materially worse off; the switch report's figure implies near-equivalence with a small cost. These cannot both be accurate projections of the same recommendation. The report does not explain or reconcile this discrepancy to the client, creating a material risk that the client has not seen the true detriment. Third, the charge comparison for Zurich contains an internal error: the report states the existing charge as 0.93%, but the derived figures note that the EA4 (Zurich) existing annual charge of £961.98 at 0.93% implies a fund value of £103,438.71 (which matches Zurich's actual current value), yet the same document's pension switch report shows Zurich only comparison with a 'Reduction in Yield' of 0.29% on the existing arrangement, not 0.93% recurring charge. This suggests the 0.93% may be overstated or computed differently. Fourth, the recommendation is justified in part on switching costs being lower with the new provider (like-for-like 0.24% cheaper), but when adviser fees of 1.00% ongoing are included, the plan becomes 4.24% more expensive. The report states this directly but does not clearly articulate why a client should accept a materially more expensive outcome, nor does it quantify the years of outperformance required to recoup the adviser fee and initial charge deduction (£3,305). The critical yield of 4.8% (or 3.2%, depending on document) is stated but not explained in client-friendly terms. Fifth, the retirement options comparison contains a spurious distinction: the report compares existing 'FAD' against recommended 'Flexi Access Drawdown with greater/enhanced flexibility', but FAD and Flexi Access Drawdown are the same thing; the suggestion that the new solution offers more flexibility is unsupported and misleading. The policy research evidence [P1] and Key Features [P7] show both allow the same drawdown options. Sixth, the Zurich retirement options are stated in the comparison as 'Full and partial UFPLS only' (no FAD), but the policy research table [P1] lists FAD as available ('1' = yes); this is a direct contradiction between the comparison assertion and the evidence. Seventh, for Standard Life, the report does not present a side-by-side fund-range or adviser-servicing comparison dimension, despite Standard Life having 300 funds and adviser charging capability already; the omission of this comparison for the smallest holding (£3,002) is not material individually but reflects selective comparison framing. Eighth, the Scottish Widows arrangement is recommended for encashment under small pots rules, but no explicit cost/benefit analysis of encashment vs retention is provided; the report shows an estimated net receipt of £170 but does not compare this outcome against retaining the funds in the new solution or another vehicle, nor does it address the tax efficiency of encashment vs retention. Ninth, performance data is presented for existing arrangements but the new solution's projected performance relative to existing Zurich is not quantified; the report asserts Zurich funds (44.32% over 5 years) have outperformed the recommended funds but does not show the recommended portfolio's 5-year performance track record. The narrative 'comparable performance with reduced volatility' is qualitative and not supported by a numeric comparison. Tenth, the report asserts the Aviva existing arrangement has 'limited range' and a lifestyling fund that will 'move outside of your agreed risk range' as retirement approaches, but the lifestyling approach is designed to reduce risk in line with proximity to retirement; the report does not evidence why this automatic de-risking is unsuitable or worse than a fixed Risk 5 allocation that may prove too risky closer to retirement. All of these discrepancies are material to the client's decision. The evidence base is substantial (pension switch report, policy research, fund factsheets), but the internal inconsistencies within and between the suitability report and switch report create genuine uncertainty about the true cost, benefit, and detriment of the recommendation. The client has not been presented with a single, consistent financial comparison that justifies consolidation. The recommendation may be sound, but the evidence pack does not demonstrate it coherently.","citations":[{"passageId":"P16","source":"Suitability Report Test 1.md","category":"I_RecommendationsAndAdvice","quote":"Charges: 0.50% fund and £4.50 annual admin charge (Aviva); 0.93% platform (Zurich); 0.52% fund charge (Standard Life)","cells":null},{"passageId":"P3","source":"Pension switch report - update